---
code: solver-consistency-plan-demoted
when-fires: a solve-certifier tripped somewhere in this run's analysis spine, so the terminal cleanup pass evicted every elision-family outcome and the plan this run emits removes nothing (`302:rul-certifier-trip-guard-only`). ONE latch per run, set by any `SolveConsistency::Inconsistent` at any of the four solve seats and in any fixpoint round, is read once immediately before plan-emission: every `Replace` (elide, and SURVIVE — an elision kept past a running wall) and every `Omit` (a fold-proved-dead branch) demotes to run, and a guard demotes too unless its verdict funcname has exactly one definition in the loaded unit. Fires once per run, spanless, whatever the count. `{demoted}` = how many sites the cleanup demoted, which legitimately reads zero when the run had nothing to evict.
why: 302 rul-certifier-trip-guard-only. A trip means the solver and the certifier DISAGREE, and the pair shares substrate (`Eq`, canonical forms, the transfer model), so the disagreement cannot distinguish a solver-class defect from a substrate-class one: it disqualifies BOTH, and neither may testify afterward. Hence a whole-scope eviction rather than a carve — there is no honest partial trust to salvage, and no recovery is planned (`302` §9: every recovery mechanism's own premises are voided by the same defect classes that fire the trip). This is a SIBLING code rather than a reason arm of solver-consistency-failure, which is the split `28L:rul-reason-enums-not-sibling-codes` sanctions -- a LICENSE variant, not a grammar fit: the cousin reports that a named solve failed its own check (an engine event, one per pass), while this reports that every license in the window has lapsed and the artifact about to be printed removes nothing (a product property, once per run). Seeing only the cousin leaves a reader not knowing what happened to their plan; seeing only this leaves them not knowing which pass to report. GUARDS may stand because `( check ) || <original>` re-verifies live on the host and the author's bytes survive verbatim as the `||`-right, so every conjunct a guard rests on is re-measured except one -- WHICH body the name resolves to -- and that one is answered by a body-occupancy census that consults no solve. SPANLESS: whole-run scope, and a caret on any book line would blame the admin's text for OUR defect (`271:rul-sin-ordering`). ERROR severity with a WarnOrDeny floor, matching its cousin: the resulting plan is valid and safe -- it is the no-Dorc floor plus whatever guards stood -- but an off-ramp `apply` must never ship a defect-affected artifact silently. Per-line demotion reasons stay pull-tier as `DemoteTag::CertifierTripped` narrative records (`rul-chain-is-pull-only`). Fixture-routed: an engine defect has no honest book trigger, since no input can arrange for the solver to be broken (`303:fnd-refusal-has-no-honest-trigger`). Remediation register (help) wanted: say plainly that this is Dorc's own defect and not the book's, that the plan is safe but poorer because nothing was removed, and that the whylog is a deterministic reproducer for a bug report.
edit-loop: edit a sentence in the transcript below, then run `mise run loom:compile -- solver-consistency-plan-demoted && mise run loom:promote -- solver-consistency-plan-demoted`; prove it stuck with `mise run test`, which is also what catches anything else the promotion moved. `mise run loom:vars -- --all solver-consistency-plan-demoted` lists this case's values; type {{name}} to insert or move one.
---
-- replay --
$ dorc plan --book=book.sh
error[solver-consistency-plan-demoted]: [unwritten:
solver-consistency-plan-demoted]
